# Execute in admin powershell
# chown
PS C:\cygwin64\home\20801921\src> $File="C:\cygwin64\home\20801921\src\monitorRoute.sh"
PS C:\cygwin64\home\20801921\src> $NewOwner = "cx\20801921"
PS C:\cygwin64\home\20801921\src> $acl = Get-Acl -Path $File
PS C:\cygwin64\home\20801921\src> $OwnerObj = new-object System.security.Principal.NTAccount($NewOwner)
PS C:\cygwin64\home\20801921\src> $acl.SetOwner($OwnerObj)
PS C:\cygwin64\home\20801921\src> Set-Acl -Path $File -AclObject $acl






Certainly! Changing the owner of a file in PowerShell can be done using the Set-ACL cmdlet along with Get-ACL and New-Object. Below are three alternatives to achieve this:

Alternative 1: Using Set-ACL and New-Object
Copy the code
# Define the file path and new owner
$filePath = "C:\path\to\your\file.txt"
$newOwner = "DOMAIN\NewOwner"

# Get the current ACL of the file
$acl = Get-Acl -Path $filePath

# Create a new owner object
$owner = New-Object System.Security.Principal.NTAccount($newOwner)

# Set the owner in the ACL
$acl.SetOwner($owner)

# Apply the new ACL to the file
Set-Acl -Path $filePath -AclObject $acl

Alternative 2: Using icacls Command
Copy the code
# Define the file path and new owner
$filePath = "C:\path\to\your\file.txt"
$newOwner = "DOMAIN\NewOwner"

# Use icacls to change the owner
icacls $filePath /setowner $newOwner /T /C

Alternative 3: Using Invoke-Expression with icacls
Copy the code
# Define the file path and new owner
$filePath = "C:\path\to\your\file.txt"
$newOwner = "DOMAIN\NewOwner"

# Construct the icacls command
$command = "icacls $filePath /setowner $newOwner /T /C"

# Execute the command
Invoke-Expression $command


Feel free to choose the method that best suits your needs. If you have any questions or need further assistance, I'm here to help!
